<!-- Section 6: How Angles Change Side Lengths -->
<section id="angle-effect" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Angle Affects the Missing Side</h2>
  <p>Fix the hypotenuse and let the angle grow: the opposite side stretches while the adjacent side shrinks. The angle acts like a dial dividing the hypotenuse between the two legs. At 0 degrees the opposite side vanishes and the adjacent leg equals the whole hypotenuse; at 90 degrees the reverse happens. Tangent behaves more dramatically because it has no hypotenuse to anchor it: as the angle nears 90 degrees, the opposite leg towers over a shrinking adjacent leg and the tangent climbs without bound.</p>
  <p>Two triangles can share the same acute angle yet differ completely in size. The angle fixes the shape, meaning all corresponding side ratios match, while the hypotenuse sets the scale. Such triangles are similar: a 5 m ladder at 65° and a 10 m ladder at 65° reach heights in exact 1:2 proportion. The table below shows this dial effect for a 10-unit hypotenuse.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[440px] border-collapse text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Angle A</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Opposite a = 10 × sin(A)</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Adjacent b = 10 × cos(A)</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">tan(A) = a / b</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4">30°</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">≈ 5.00</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">≈ 8.66</td><td class="py-2">≈ 0.577</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2 pr-4">45°</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">≈ 7.07</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">≈ 7.07</td><td class="py-2">≈ 1.000</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15 last:border-0"><td class="py-2 pr-4">60°</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">≈ 8.66</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">≈ 5.00</td><td class="py-2">≈ 1.732</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>At 45° the legs balance exactly and the tangent equals 1. Notice the symmetry: the 30° row mirrors the 60° row with legs swapped, because the side opposite 30° is adjacent to 60°.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: Degrees and Calculator Settings -->
<section id="angle-degrees" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why the Correct Angle Setting Matters</h2>
  <p>Angles can be measured in degrees or radians, and the two scales disagree completely on what a number means. A full circle is 360 degrees but only about 6.283 radians. This calculator expects degrees: entering 30 means 30°, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(30^\\circ) = 0.5]]')} />. If the same 30 were read as radians, the sine would be about −0.988, a wildly different answer with no warning.</p>
  <p>The trap works the other way too. Suppose your angle comes from a calculation that produced radians, say 0.5 rad (about 28.65°). Typing 0.5 into a degree-mode calculator computes sin(0.5°) ≈ 0.0087 instead of sin(0.5 rad) ≈ 0.479. Always confirm which unit your angle is in before entering it. Rounding the angle early also costs accuracy: at steep angles a tenth of a degree moves the tangent noticeably, so keep the full angle precision until the final step.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: How to Use the Calculator -->
<section id="angle-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Guide to Using the Calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Confirm the right triangle.</strong> Find the 90-degree corner in your sketch or situation; without it, these formulas do not apply.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify angle A.</strong> Choose the acute angle your measurements reference. The other acute angle is 90° minus this one.</li>
    <li><strong>Label the sides.</strong> Mark the hypotenuse c, then label a as the leg opposite A and b as the leg adjacent to A.</li>
    <li><strong>Note which side you know.</strong> Is it the hypotenuse or one of the legs? This single fact narrows the choice to two modes.</li>
    <li><strong>Select the matching mode.</strong> Hypotenuse known and leg a missing: Leg a From Sine. Hypotenuse known and leg b missing: Leg b From Cosine. Leg b known and leg a missing: Leg a From Tangent. Leg a known and leg b missing: Leg b From Tangent.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the side length and its unit.</strong> Use the same unit family you will report the answer in.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter angle A in degrees.</strong> Double-check it is acute, strictly between 0° and 90°.</li>
    <li><strong>Calculate.</strong> The missing side appears with solution steps showing the substitution.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the steps.</strong> Confirm the calculator used the ratio you intended and the numbers match your inputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Sanity-check the result.</strong> A leg must be shorter than the hypotenuse. If you solved from the hypotenuse, both legs from sine and cosine should satisfy a² + b² = c².</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If the interface offers a reset control, use it to clear the fields before starting a fresh calculation so no stale value leaks into the next result.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: Common Mistakes -->
<section id="angle-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Finding Triangle Sides From Angles</h2>
  <p><strong>1. Confusing the opposite and adjacent sides.</strong> Labelling is relative to angle A, and swapping the two legs swaps sine with cosine. Fix: write "opp" and "adj" on your sketch before touching the calculator.</p>
  <p><strong>2. Choosing sine when tangent is required.</strong> With a known leg and no hypotenuse in sight, sine has nothing to grip. If your known side is a leg, the answer involves tangent. Example: b = 8 m, A = 35° needs a = 8·tan(35°), not 8·sin(35°).</p>
  <p><strong>3. Using the wrong tangent formula.</strong> Finding the opposite leg multiplies by tangent; finding the adjacent leg divides by it. Memorise the direction: a = b·tan(A) grows the base into the height, b = a/tan(A) shrinks the height back into the base.</p>
  <p><strong>4. Entering the angle in the wrong unit.</strong> A radian value typed as degrees (or vice versa) silently produces garbage. Confirm degrees first; this calculator works in degrees.</p>
  <p><strong>5. Treating the hypotenuse as an ordinary leg.</strong> The hypotenuse only pairs with sine and cosine, never with tangent. If your formula has tan and a hypotenuse, something is mislabelled.</p>
  <p><strong>6. Using the wrong reference angle.</strong> The 65° in the ladder example is measured from the ground. Measuring from the wall instead would make it 25°, and every ratio would change. Always check which side the angle opens from.</p>
  <p><strong>7. Mixing metres, feet, or other units.</strong> A hypotenuse in feet with an answer expected in metres needs conversion before or after, never skipped. Keep one unit through the whole calculation.</p>
  <p><strong>8. Rounding intermediate values too early.</strong> Rounding tan(35°) to 0.7 before multiplying by 8 gives 5.6 by luck here, but coarser rounding elsewhere drifts the answer. Keep full precision until the final result.</p>
  <p><strong>9. Assuming the side opposite an angle is always the longest.</strong> The opposite side is only longest when the angle exceeds 45°. At 30°, the opposite leg is the shortest side of the triangle.</p>
  <p><strong>10. Using trigonometry on a non-right triangle.</strong> Without a 90° angle, sine, cosine, and tangent as defined here do not apply. Obtuse and acute triangles need the Laws of Sines and Cosines instead.</p>
  <p><strong>11. Entering an angle outside the acute range.</strong> Angles of 0°, 90°, or more collapse the triangle or describe a different shape. The calculator needs an angle strictly between 0° and 90°.</p>
  <p><strong>12. Mistrusting a small but correct output.</strong> A 5° ramp over 12 m genuinely rises only about 1.05 m. Small angles produce small opposite sides; that is the geometry, not an error.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: Input Validation -->
<section id="angle-validation" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Checking Whether Your Result Is Mathematically Valid</h2>
  <p>Side lengths must be positive numbers; a zero or negative length describes no triangle at all. Any leg you calculate must come out shorter than the hypotenuse you entered. The reference angle must be acute, strictly between 0° and 90°, and every length in the problem must share one unit.</p>
  <p>Tangent deserves extra respect near the edges of its range. As the angle approaches 90°, the tangent climbs steeply, so a tiny wobble in the angle swings the result dramatically. Near 0°, the tangent is tiny, and dividing by it (the b = a/tan(A) mode) magnifies any measurement noise in the same way. When your angle sits within a few degrees of either extreme, treat the inputs as approximate and the output as sensitive rather than exact.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Verifying Your Answer -->
<section id="angle-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Your Answer</h2>
  <p><strong>Method 1: Substitute back into the ratio.</strong> Divide your answer by the known side and confirm the ratio matches the trigonometric value. If you found a = 5 from c = 10 at 30°, check that 5/10 = 0.5 = sin(30°).</p>
  <p><strong>Method 2: Use the Pythagorean theorem.</strong> When you know two sides after the calculation, verify that a² + b² = c². This cross-checks trigonometry against pure geometry.</p>
  <p><strong>Method 3: Check relative sizes.</strong> Every leg must be shorter than the hypotenuse. The leg opposite the larger acute angle must be the longer leg. At 30°, expect the opposite leg to be the shorter one.</p>
  <p><strong>Worked verification:</strong> from c = 10 m and A = 30°, the calculator gives a = 5 m. Find the other leg: b = 10·cos(30°) ≈ 8.66 m. Now check Pythagoras with unrounded values:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-4 rounded-xl border border-outline-variant/25 bg-surface-container-lowest/60 p-5" set:html={rich('[[[5^2 + (10\\cos 30^\\circ)^2 = 25 + 75 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />
  <p>The equality holds, the hypotenuse is longest, and 5 &lt; 8.66 matches the 30° geometry. Any tiny mismatch comes from rounding the 8.66, so verify with unrounded values when precision matters.</p>
</section>

